now votes are being counted in kenya  after a lower than expected turnout in  tuesday's general election voters are  selecting a new president and choosing  members of the country's parliament  early indicators are showing a tight  race between the two main candidates  deputy president william ruto and  veteran opposition leader raila odinga  are both projected to having close to 50  of the vote and that means a runoff  election may be needed  and for more on this we can cross  straight to nairobi now and speak to our  correspondent there edith kimani hi  edith the the turnout in this election  was really quite low especially among  younger voters tell us why that was  well if i was to summarize it into one  word it would be money a lot of people  here in kenya are simply grappling with  just how to survive on a regular basis  there was a viral video that went out  about a month ago of a lady who went to  the supermarket and upon realizing that  the price of maizeflower which is a  staple here in kenya was two dollars up  from one dollar and 20 cents she just  started screaming and almost sort of  lost her mind one might describe and  this is sort of what is on the mind of  most canyons every day i have people  asking me for money ask me for jobs and  this is not unique to me personally this  is just a state of affairs in the  country and this is what is on the minds  of people and so an election doesn't  seem so important  so a lot of pressure on the person who  will be president next can you tell us  about the two men who are running for  president and what policies we could  expect from each of them  railaudinger being uh the veteran in uh  this race he is 77 years old certainly  not a new face in kenyan politics he has  held various positions for member of  parliament right up to uh prime minister  he is actually saying that he's tried  and tested hands that's his promise that  he's going to sort out issues of health  care that he's going to finally end  corruption which is endemic in this  country and the thing that is most  important to most uh to kenya's  listening to him is that he's going to  provide a 50 stipend for those who do  not have a salary or who don't have jobs  uh william ruto his contender is  newer in the political scene by no means  a rookie he is himself also very much in  the kenyan political game has been for a  while he's held various positions right  up to the deputy president and his uh  promise is that he is going to put the  grassroots people first the the local  man he's saying that he's going to have  a bottom-up approach that's what he's  calling it and so both of these  contenders are saying that they're going  to put the kenyan first and what is  ironic is that most kenyans feel that  they're not being placed anywhere in  this election  so whoever does win what needs to be  their number one priority  absolutely looking at what's happening  with the state of the economy as i  mentioned earlier the quality of life  here for most kenyans is exceptionally  poor of course that has been made worse  by the war in ukraine uh we're still  reeling from the effects of the global  pandemic that we are still actually  experiencing and there's a drought that  is currently ongoing so that will have  to be top of the agenda uh for the  person who does come in to take over the  khamani  government  nairobi thank you so much
 

